/* Jesus in the OT › Psalms, Zechariah 9:9, Matthew's fulfilment formula and related NT uses of the OT — sources: Troki, Kravitz, Choose Life, Discord (2026-11-24) */
(()=>{
const {cr,N,card}=window.OTJ;

const D110a={server:'IA',channel:'📖・seerahpro-bible-notes',id:'1514369926444617758'};
const D110b={server:'DR',channel:'jesus-is-called-lord-debunked',id:'1368590745375608922'};
const D110c={server:'DR',channel:'mark',id:'1396880233910632508'};
const DM1={server:'IA',channel:'📖・muhammad-ﷺ-in-the-bible',id:'1515609239777972345'};
const DROCK={server:'AV',channel:'moses-sprechender-stein',id:'1289901959930839050'};

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ PSALM 110 */
card('psalm-110',{
  title:"“The LORD said to my Lord” (Psalm 110)",
  k:"Psalm 110:1–4; Mark 12:35–37",
  blurb:"David’s “my lord” is adoni, a title for a human master, not Adonai. The seat at the right hand is a royal honour.",
  claim:"Psalm 110 shows David calling the Messiah his divine Lord, who sits beside God and is a priest like Melchizedek.",
  tree:N(cr('troki',D110a,D110b,D110c),
    "“The LORD said to my Lord, sit at my right hand.” David calls someone “my Lord” who sits beside God. Jesus uses this verse in Mark 12:35–37 to show that the Messiah is more than David’s son. The “priest for ever after the order of Melchizedek” points to a new priesthood.",
    "The two “Lords” are different words in Hebrew. The first is YHWH. The second is adoni, “my master”, a normal title for kings and men. The Greek word kyrios hides this difference. A seat at the right hand is a royal honour, not a claim to deity.",
    "The Christian case at its strongest, and the core answer",
    [ "Steelman · Whom would David call “my Lord”? David was the king. He had no human superior in Israel, so the one he calls lord must be greater than a king (Troki, Part I ch. 40).",
      "Steelman · Jesus himself quotes the verse and asks how David’s lord can also be David’s son (Mark 12:35–37; Discord, DR #mark).",
      "Steelman · Being seated at God’s right hand, and being a priest “for ever” in the line of Melchizedek, are said to describe a divine king-priest who replaces the old sacrifices (Troki, Part I ch. 40).",
      "Answer · Hebrew has three words. YHWH is God’s name. Adonai is “my Lord” said of God. Adon, or adoni with “my”, is a human master. Psalm 110:1 uses adoni, the human word. Abraham and David are also called adon. (Discord, DR #jesus-is-called-lord-debunked)",
      "Answer · The Greek translation (LXX) renders both YHWH and adon as kyrios, so it reads “the kyrios said to my kyrios”. The only difference in the Greek is a case ending. Greek alone cannot tell you whether divine or human lordship is meant. (Discord, DR #jesus-is-called-lord-debunked)",
      "Answer · Sitting at the right hand is a position of honour. In 1 Kings 2:19 Bathsheba is seated at the right hand of King Solomon, and nobody says she is divine. (Discord, IA #seerahpro-bible-notes)",
      "Answer · Troki reads “right hand” as power and protection (Ex 15:6; Ps 118:16). To sit at God’s right hand is to be under his protection, not to share his nature. (Troki, Part I ch. 40)",
      "Turn the tables · Christians who take “right hand” literally give God a body, and then place the Son beside him. The text does not need that reading. (Troki, Part I ch. 40)"],
    ["Psalm 110:1–4","1 Kings 2:19","Mark 12:35–37","Troki, Faith Strengthened I.40","Discord IA #seerahpro-bible-notes","Discord DR #jesus-is-called-lord-debunked","Discord DR #mark"],
    [
      N(cr(D110b),
        "But the New Testament calls Jesus kyrios again and again. That is the same word the Greek Bible uses for YHWH.",
        "Kyrios was an ordinary Greek word for “sir, master, lord”. It was also used for people. Reading it as YHWH every time is a choice made before reading, not something the word proves.",
        "Kyrios does not prove YHWH",
        [ "The LXX uses kyrios for adon, Adonai and YHWH. So the word cannot tell you which Hebrew title stands behind it. (Discord, DR #jesus-is-called-lord-debunked)",
          "The author of the Discord thread says the case for reading kyrios of Jesus as YHWH is a preconceived reading. He also says that the lexicon entries (Strong’s 113 adon, BDB) that are cited for Adonai as a divine name mostly belong to Strong’s 136, not 113. He notes he checked five entries, not all. Treat this point as a lead to verify. (Discord, DR #jesus-is-called-lord-debunked)",
          "For Psalm 110 the Hebrew is not in doubt: the second title is adoni, so the Greek form does not change what David wrote. (Discord, DR #jesus-is-called-lord-debunked; LXX Ps 109:1)"],
        ["LXX Psalm 110:1 (109:1)","Strong’s 113, 136, 3068","Brown-Driver-Briggs","Discord DR #jesus-is-called-lord-debunked"],
        [],
        {visual:{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What English and Greek show',items:['“The Lord said to my Lord”','Greek: kyrios … kyriō (one word, two cases)','Looks like the same title twice']},right:{title:'What the Hebrew shows',items:['YHWH said to adoni (“my master”)','Adon = human master (Abraham, David also)','Adonai/YHWH = God, a different word']}}}
      ),
      N(cr('troki'),
        "But the psalm cannot be about David himself. David is the speaker, and “my Lord” is someone else.",
        "Troki reads the psalm as being about David. The speaker can be David’s own men, or the psalmist, speaking to David. Many psalms are written about the poet or about the king, under inspiration.",
        "Psalm 110 about David",
        [ "Many psalms speak about the poet or the king and are written under inspiration (Ps 2:7; 137; 79; 74). Psalm 110 fits that pattern. (Troki, Part I ch. 40)",
          "Troki’s reading: the voice is that of David’s men, who swore he should not go out to battle again (2 Sam 21:17). They tell him to sit safe at God’s right hand until God subdues his enemies. “Thy people shall be willing in the day of thy power” means his men offering their lives. This is one reading, offered by Troki, not a point everyone accepts. (Troki, Part I ch. 40)",
          "The verse about swearing and not repenting (v. 4) cannot mean that a new priesthood replaces the sacrifices. God does not change (Num 23:19), and the law binds all generations. (Troki, Part I ch. 40)"],
        ["Psalm 110","2 Samuel 21:17","Numbers 23:19","Troki, Faith Strengthened I.40"],
        []
      ),
      N(cr('troki'),
        "Then what about Melchizedek? Bread and wine, a priest-king, and a priesthood “for ever”.",
        "The verse says “according to my word”, and David is both king and priest-like. Melchizedek was king of Salem and priest of the Most High. David too is a king who sacrifices and prays. The bread and wine were hospitality for Abraham’s men.",
        "Melchizedek",
        [ "The Hebrew phrase translated “after the order of” is al divrati. Troki reads it as “according to”, in the manner of Melchizedek, a king who is also priest. (Troki, Part I ch. 40)",
          "David acted as a priest-like figure: he sacrificed and prayed, for example in 2 Samuel 24. (Troki, Part I ch. 40)",
          "Melchizedek brought bread and wine for Abraham’s party (Gen 14:18). Troki says this was hospitality and that Abraham’s tithe does not turn it into a prefiguring of the Eucharist. (Troki, Part I ch. 40)"],
        ["Genesis 14:18–20","Psalm 110:4","2 Samuel 24","Troki, Faith Strengthened I.40"],
        []
      ),
      N(cr(D110b),
        "The Church fathers said the pre-incarnate Christ was the Angel of the LORD. Doesn’t that make him YHWH?",
        "Turn the tables: if the Son was the Angel who appears in a form, he is contained, and YHWH cannot be contained (1 Kings 8:27). Then he lacks something YHWH must have.",
        "Turn the tables · the contained Angel",
        [ "Turn the tables · The Discord thread argues: if the pre-incarnate Jesus is the Angel of the LORD, as early fathers say, he is contained in a form. Solomon says the LORD cannot be contained (1 Kgs 8:27). So he lacks a necessary quality of YHWH. (Discord, DR #jesus-is-called-lord-debunked)"],
        ["1 Kings 8:27","Discord DR #jesus-is-called-lord-debunked"],
        []
      ),
    ],
    {quotes:[{by:'Isaac Troki',en:'“Sit at my right hand” means seek the protection of the Almighty.',ref:'Faith Strengthened I.40 (paraphrase)'}]}
  ),
  sum:"Psalm 110:1 has two different words for “Lord”. The second is adoni, a human master. The seat at the right hand is a royal honour (1 Kings 2:19). The Greek kyrios hides the difference."
});

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ PSALM 2 */
card('psalm-2',{
  title:"“Kiss the Son” (Psalm 2)",
  k:"Psalm 2:7, 12; 2 Samuel 7:14",
  blurb:"Psalm 2 is a coronation psalm for David. “Son” is a covenant title for Israel and its kings.",
  claim:"Psalm 2 presents the divine Messiah as God’s Son, whom the nations must kiss (worship), and Acts and Hebrews apply it to Jesus.",
  tree:N(cr('troki','cl'),
    "“Thou art my Son, this day have I begotten thee.” Acts 13:33 and Hebrews 1:5 quote Psalm 2:7 as proof that Jesus is the Son of God. Verse 12 tells the nations to “kiss the Son”, so the Son is worshipped.",
    "In the Old Testament, “son of God” is a title for Israel and for its king. The psalm is about David’s accession. Verse 12 is hard to translate, and it is not even needed for the Christian point. The psalm also does not fit Jesus.",
    "The Christian case at its strongest, and the core answer",
    [ "Steelman · Verse 7 says “Thou art my Son”, spoken to the anointed king. The New Testament applies it to Jesus (Acts 13:33; Heb 1:5; Troki, Part II ch. 68, 95).",
      "Steelman · The psalm describes all nations under the Son’s rule, and says that those who take refuge in him are blessed. This sounds bigger than any Israelite king (Choose Life, Tampering with the Evidence, Example #4).",
      "Steelman · 2 Samuel 7:14 (“I will be his father, and he shall be my son”) gives the same pattern of God as Father to David’s heir (Troki, Part II ch. 95).",
      "Answer · The psalm is about David. The nations raged against him when he began to reign (2 Sam 5:17). “I have anointed my king” fits 1 Samuel 16:1, 13. Zion was David’s city, and he subdued Philistia, Ammon, Moab and Edom (2 Sam 8:1), which fits “ask of me, and I shall give the nations”. (Troki, Part II ch. 68)",
      "Answer · “Son” and “begotten” are covenant language, and are used of Israel: Israel is God’s firstborn son (Ex 4:22), the “sons of the living God” (Hos 2:1), and God “begat” Israel (Deut 32:18). David was changed by God’s Spirit when anointed (1 Sam 16:13). (Troki, Part II ch. 68)",
      "Answer · Verse 12 has a hard Hebrew word, bar. Choose Life reads it as the Hebrew bar, “purity”, so “embrace purity”, and not the Aramaic bar “son”. The translation is disputed. Even if “son” is correct there, it is a title of the king, as in verse 7. (Choose Life, Tampering with the Evidence p. 22)",
      "Answer · Context is a warning to the rulers of the world about their behaviour. It is not a prediction about a divine Messiah. (Choose Life, p. 22)"],
    ["Psalm 2:7, 12","2 Samuel 7:14","Exodus 4:22","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part II ch. 68, 95","Choose Life, Tampering with the Evidence Example #4, p. 22"],
    [
      N(cr('cl'),
        "But isn’t “kiss the Son” clear? The Hebrew says bar, and bar means “son” in Aramaic, as in bar mitzvah.",
        "Bar is Aramaic for “son”, but this is a Hebrew psalm, and Hebrew has its own word, ben, which verse 7 uses. Why would David switch to Aramaic for one word? A different reading is available.",
        "The word bar",
        [ "Choose Life renders the verse “embrace [worship in] purity, lest He be angry”. Bar also means pure or clean in Hebrew. (Choose Life, Tampering with the Evidence Example #4, p. 22)",
          "The Christian reading needs bar to be Aramaic “son”. Choose Life asks why an Aramaic word would appear in a Hebrew psalm of David. This is a fair question. It is not a proof, since the verse is among the harder ones in the Psalter. (Choose Life, p. 22)",
          "Fairness: the Christian argument about “Son” does not need verse 12, because verse 7 already says “my son”. So the stronger reply is that “son” in verse 7 is the king’s covenant title, as shown above."],
        ["Psalm 2:7, 12","Choose Life, Tampering with the Evidence p. 22"],
        []
      ),
      N(cr('troki'),
        "“This day have I begotten thee” must mean a real divine Son.",
        "“Begotten” here is adoption into the covenant on the day of the anointing, the same word picture used of Israel. If Jesus were God, the psalm would not have him “ask” for the nations. They would already be his.",
        "“Begotten” and “ask of me”",
        [ "Israel is told that God “begat” them (Deut 32:18). So “begotten” can describe a covenant act toward a people. (Troki, Part II ch. 68)",
          "Turn the tables · If Jesus were God and Creator, he would already own the earth, and he would not be invited to “ask” for nations. The Psalm also gives him a rod of iron and dominion. Jesus said he came to serve (Matt 20:28) and he held no worldly dominion. (Troki, Part II ch. 68)",
          "Turn the tables · Acts 13:33 applies “this day have I begotten thee” to the day of the resurrection. A day on which someone is begotten is a day on which he becomes Son. That is the language of a new status, not of an eternal Son. (adapted from Acts 13:33 text)"],
        ["Psalm 2:7–8","Acts 13:33","Matthew 20:28","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part II ch. 68"],
        []
      ),
      N(cr('troki'),
        "What about 2 Samuel 7:14, “I will be his father”? That is a promise about the Messiah.",
        "It is a promise about David’s son Solomon. The same verse says that if he does wrong, God will correct him. Christians do not apply that to Jesus.",
        "2 Samuel 7:14",
        [ "Turn the tables · The clause “if he commit iniquity, I will chasten him” belongs to the same verse. It cannot be said of a sinless Jesus. So the verse is about a human king of David’s line. (Troki, Part II ch. 95)",
          "Israel itself is God’s firstborn (Ex 4:22), so the title is not unique to one person. (Troki, Part II ch. 68)"],
        ["2 Samuel 7:14","Exodus 4:22","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part II ch. 95"],
        []
      ),
    ]
  ),
  sum:"Psalm 2 is a coronation psalm for David. “Son” and “begotten” are covenant titles used of Israel and its king, and verse 12 (bar) is a disputed word."
});

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ PSALM 45 */
card('psalm-45',{
  title:"“Your throne, O God” (Psalm 45)",
  k:"Psalm 45:6–7; Hebrews 1:8–9",
  blurb:"Elohim is used of judges and kings. And the next verse says “God, your God, has anointed you”.",
  claim:"Hebrews 1:8 quotes Psalm 45:6 to show the Son is addressed as God.",
  tree:N(cr('troki'),
    "Psalm 45:6 says, “Thy throne, O God, is for ever and ever.” Hebrews 1:8 applies this to the Son. So the Son is called God in the Old Testament.",
    "The psalm is a wedding song for an Israelite king. The word elohim is used of humans in the Bible, such as judges. And the next verse says the one addressed has a God of his own who anointed him.",
    "The Christian case at its strongest, and the core answer",
    [ "Steelman · The Hebrew can be read as a direct address, “Thy throne, O God”, and Hebrews 1:8 takes it that way (Troki, Part II ch. 95).",
      "Steelman · The Epistle follows with “let all the angels of God worship him” (Heb 1:6), so the Son is placed above angels and worshipped (Troki, Part II ch. 94–95).",
      "Answer · The very next verse says, “God, even thy God, hath anointed thee”. If the one addressed were God, the Psalm could not say that someone is his God. (Troki, Part II ch. 95)",
      "Answer · Another reading is “thy throne is of God”, or “thy throne is God’s”, as David’s dynasty occupies God’s throne. Solomon sat “on the throne of the LORD” (1 Chr 29:23). (Troki, Part II ch. 95; this is a possible reading, not the only one)",
      "Answer · Elohim is not a name reserved for God. The Bible uses it of judges and rulers (Ex 21:6; 22:8–9; Ps 82:6), and Moses is made “a god” to Pharaoh (Ex 7:1). So a human king can be addressed with it."],
    ["Psalm 45:6–7","1 Chronicles 29:23","Hebrews 1:6–9","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part II ch. 94–95"],
    [
      N(cr('troki'),
        "Hebrews 1:6 says “let all the angels of God worship him”. That proves the Son is worshipped.",
        "Hebrews 1:6 draws on Psalm 97:7, where “worship him, all ye gods” is addressed to the LORD, the Lord of all the earth. It is not a verse about a son. Hebrews here shifts a text about God to someone else.",
        "Psalm 97:7 and Hebrews 1:6",
        [ "Psalm 97:7 is addressed to God, Lord of the whole earth. It is not about Jesus. (Troki, Part II ch. 94–95)",
          "Troki adds that the Epistle to the Hebrews is anonymous and was rejected early as apocryphal. This is a weaker point and I would not lean on it: who wrote Hebrews, and its early reception, is debated. (Troki, Part II ch. 94–95)"],
        ["Psalm 97:7","Hebrews 1:6","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part II ch. 94–95"],
        []
      ),
      N(cr('troki'),
        "Even so, Hebrews 1:8 is in the New Testament. Doesn’t it add its own interpretation?",
        "Turn the tables: Hebrews 1:9 continues in the same breath, “therefore God, even thy God, hath anointed thee.” The author keeps the same line that says the Son has a God.",
        "Turn the tables · Hebrews 1:9",
        [ "Turn the tables · Hebrews 1:8–9 itself repeats the next verse of the psalm: “God, even thy God, hath anointed thee”. If the Son is God by nature, then who is his God? (Troki, Part II ch. 95)",
          "Turn the tables · If elohim in the psalm can only mean the one true God, then Psalm 82:6 (“Ye are gods”) and Exodus 7:1 would prove the same of judges and Moses. The Bible has a wide use of the word. (adapted from the Bible texts above)"],
        ["Hebrews 1:8–9","Psalm 82:6","Exodus 7:1","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part II ch. 95"],
        []
      ),
    ]
  ),
  sum:"Psalm 45 is a king’s wedding song. Elohim is used of humans and judges, and verse 7 says that “God, thy God” anointed the one addressed."
});

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ PSALM 16:10 */
card('psalm-16-10',{
  title:"“You will not abandon my soul to Sheol” (Psalm 16:10)",
  k:"Psalm 16:10; Acts 2:25–31; Acts 13:35–37",
  blurb:"Peter and Paul read David’s confidence as a prediction of Jesus’ resurrection. The Hebrew speaks of the pit, and of David himself.",
  claim:"Psalm 16:10 predicts that the Messiah would not see corruption, and Peter and Paul say this was fulfilled in Jesus’ resurrection.",
  tree:N(cr('troki'),
    "In Acts 2 and Acts 13 the apostles say David died and “saw corruption”, so the verse cannot be about David. It must be about Jesus, whose body did not decay.",
    "The Hebrew word is shachat, which usually means “pit” or grave. David is speaking about his own confidence that God will not leave his soul to the pit. The Greek word “corruption” in Acts leans on a translation. The verse expresses hope of the righteous, not a resurrection prediction.",
    "The Christian case at its strongest, and the core answer",
    [ "Steelman · Peter’s argument: David died and is buried, and his tomb is with us, so “thy Holy One shall not see corruption” cannot have been fulfilled in David. Jesus rose before decay (Acts 2:29–31; 13:35–37; Troki, Part II ch. 69).",
      "Answer · The Hebrew shachat means a pit, as in Ps 94:13, Prov 26:27 and Ps 7:16. In those verses it is a pit or grave. Troki says David’s point is that only the body goes down to the pit and his soul is not left to perdition. The Hebrew word can also be linked to “corruption”, and translators disagree, so this is Troki’s reading of the word, not a settled point. (Troki, Part II ch. 69)",
      "Answer · The verse is about a hasid, a loyal, righteous person, and David says it of himself in a psalm of trust in God. Troki himself reads Ps 16:10–11 as proof that the soul of the righteous is not abandoned and has “a path of life”. (Troki, Part I ch. 18)",
      "Turn the tables · Peter’s argument needs a Greek wording about “corruption” (decay of the body). Speakers of Hebrew, reading “pit”, would not hear the same claim. The verse, then, says nothing about a body rising on the third day. (Troki, Part II ch. 69)"],
    ["Psalm 16:8–11","Psalm 7:16; 94:13; Proverbs 26:27","Acts 2:25–31; 13:35–37","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part I ch. 18; Part II ch. 69"],
    [
      N(cr('troki'),
        "David could not have meant himself, because David did decay.",
        "Troki’s point is that David speaks about the soul and about trust, not about a body that never decays. The same Psalm says the soul of the loyal is not left to the pit. This is the hope of every righteous person.",
        "David’s own hope",
        [ "The psalm opens with David’s trust: “in thee do I put my trust”, and ends with “a path of life” and joy. This is the voice of a man trusting God in his own life. (Psalm 16:1, 11; Troki, Part I ch. 18)",
          "If it were a prophecy that the body would not rot, the Hebrew would say so clearly. Instead the verse says the soul is not abandoned to the pit. (Troki, Part II ch. 69)",
          "Turn the tables · Acts 2:29 itself says David’s tomb is “with us unto this day”. Peter concedes that David is speaking in the first person, and then reinterprets the words as speaking of someone else. (adapted from Acts 2:29–31)"],
        ["Psalm 16:1, 10–11","Acts 2:29","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part I ch. 18; Part II ch. 69"],
        []
      ),
    ]
  ),
  sum:"Psalm 16:10 is David’s trust that God will not leave his soul to the pit. “Corruption” depends on how shachat is read, and the verse speaks of the loyal worshipper, not of a resurrection on the third day."
});

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ PSALM 40 / HEBREWS 10 */
card('psalm-40-hebrews-10',{
  title:"“A body you prepared for me” (Psalm 40:6 and Hebrews 10:5)",
  k:"Psalm 40:6–8; Hebrews 10:5–10",
  blurb:"Hebrews quotes the Greek Septuagint, “a body you prepared”. The Hebrew says “ears you have opened”.",
  claim:"Hebrews 10:5 quotes Psalm 40 as the words of Christ, “a body you prepared for me”, showing the Old Testament itself expected animal sacrifice to be replaced.",
  tree:N(cr('troki','kravitz','kwta'),
    "Hebrews 10:5 puts Psalm 40 in Christ’s mouth: “Sacrifice and offering you did not desire, but a body you prepared for me.” So the Old Testament itself says sacrifice is to be replaced by Christ’s body.",
    "The Hebrew of Psalm 40:6 does not say “body”. It says “ears you have opened”. The point of the psalm is obedience. “A body” comes from the Greek Septuagint, which is what Hebrews quotes.",
    "The Christian case at its strongest, and the core answer",
    [ "Steelman · Hebrews says God did not want sacrifice and offering, and that a body was prepared, so that Christ comes to do God’s will (Heb 10:5–10; Kravitz, Claim #2).",
      "Steelman · Psalm 40 itself says God does not desire sacrifice and offering. So the Old Testament already looks beyond animal sacrifice (Kravitz, KWTA p. 10).",
      "Answer · The Hebrew reads “my ears you have opened; burnt offering and sin offering you did not require”. It is about hearing and obeying God. (Troki, Part I ch. 45; Kravitz, Claim #2, point 2c)",
      "Answer · The theme is the same as 1 Samuel 15:22, “to obey is better than sacrifice”, and Jeremiah 7:22–23 and Exodus 19:5. God wants obedience first. (Troki, Part I ch. 45; Kravitz, Claim #2, point 2c)",
      "Answer · Troki says sacrifices were ordained as medicine to draw sinners to the Temple and to mercy, not as ends in themselves. Obedience was always preferred. (Troki, Part I ch. 45)",
      "Answer · “Body” is an allusion read into the text. The Hebrew psalm gives the Christian argument nothing to build on. (Kravitz, KWTA p. 10 fn. 7)"],
    ["Psalm 40:6–8","1 Samuel 15:22","Hebrews 10:5","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part I ch. 45; Part II ch. 98","Kravitz, Jewish Response to Missionaries, Claim #2 point 2c","Kravitz, Know What to Answer p. 10 fn. 7"],
    [
      N(cr('kravitz','troki'),
        "Perhaps the author of Hebrews just mistranslated, or a missionary changed the verse.",
        "Fix the source: this is not a missionary mistake. Hebrews quotes the Septuagint, the old Greek translation, which reads “a body”. The translators of that Greek version probably worked from a Hebrew text we do not have in front of us, or paraphrased. Either way, the Hebrew text we have says “ears”.",
        "Hebrews follows the Septuagint",
        [ "Kravitz presents “body” as a missionary mistranslation. It is more accurate to say the writer of Hebrews followed the Greek Septuagint wording. (Kravitz, Claim #2 point 2c)",
          "Troki calls the Hebrews quotation a mistranslation and says Psalm 40:7 (Hebrew numbering) teaches that obedience is the chief duty and hearkening is better than the fat of rams. (Troki, Part II ch. 98)",
          "Turn the tables · Hebrews 10:5–10 builds its argument on the word “body”: “a body you prepared”, and then “through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ” (v. 10). Without that Greek word the argument has no support. The Hebrew psalm cannot be used for it. (adapted from the Hebrews 10 wording)",
          "Turn the tables · If a Christian says the Greek is inspired and the Hebrew is not, then the case depends on a translation, not on what the prophet David said."],
        ["Psalm 40:6–8","Hebrews 10:5–10","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part II ch. 98","Kravitz, Claim #2 point 2c"],
        [],
        {visual:{kind:'compare',left:{title:'Hebrews 10:5 (Greek Septuagint)',items:['“A body you prepared for me”','Used to argue that Christ’s body ends sacrifice']},right:{title:'Psalm 40:6 (Hebrew)',items:['“My ears you have opened”','God wants hearing and obedience (1 Sam 15:22)']}}}
      ),
    ]
  ),
  sum:"Hebrews 10:5 quotes the Greek “a body you prepared”. The Hebrew of Psalm 40:6 says “ears you have opened”, a call to obedience, so the argument about Christ’s body does not come from the Hebrew."
});

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ ZECHARIAH 9:9 */
card('zechariah-9-9',{
  title:"The king on a donkey (Zechariah 9:9)",
  k:"Zechariah 9:9–10; Matthew 21:1–7",
  blurb:"The whole prophecy is about an end-time ingathering and peace for the nations. And Matthew puts Jesus on two animals.",
  claim:"Zechariah 9:9, “thy king cometh … lowly, and riding upon an ass”, is fulfilled in Jesus’ entry into Jerusalem (Matthew 21:5).",
  tree:N(cr('troki'),
    "Zechariah says a righteous king comes “having salvation” and riding on a donkey. Jesus rode a donkey into Jerusalem on Palm Sunday, and the crowd called him king. Matthew says this happened to fulfil the prophecy.",
    "Read the whole chapter. Zechariah 9 is about Israel’s end-time ingathering, peace for the nations, and the end of war chariots. None of that happened in Jesus’ time. Riding a donkey proves nothing by itself, and Matthew’s version has an odd detail.",
    "The Christian case at its strongest, and the core answer",
    [ "Steelman · The king is humble (ani), just, and arrives on a donkey, not a war horse. Jesus did exactly that, in front of crowds who hailed him (Matt 21:5; Troki, Part I ch. 35).",
      "Steelman · The verse is said to name a king who brings salvation, which suits a Saviour (Troki, Part I ch. 35).",
      "Answer · The context is the whole chapter, 9:1–17. Hadrach, Damascus, Tyre and Philistia are absorbed into Israel, Judah and Ephraim return, and Javan is defeated. None of this happened in Jesus’ time. (Troki, Part I ch. 35)",
      "Answer · Verse 10 belongs to the same prophecy: chariots and horses are cut off, “he shall speak peace unto the nations”, and his dominion is “from sea to sea”. Jesus said he came with a sword (Matt 10:34) and to serve (Matt 20:28), and held no worldly dominion. (Troki, Part I ch. 35)",
      "Answer · The Hebrew word nosha is a passive form, commonly rendered “saved” or “victorious”, and the English “having salvation” can mislead. Troki reads it as the king who is himself saved, not a saviour of others. (Troki, Part I ch. 35)",
      "Answer · A donkey signals humility and the end of war horses, and it shows what the king is like, not who he is. Anyone could ride a donkey. Troki lists five points of Zechariah 9 that are still unfulfilled: the gathering of Israel, nations joining, Gog and Magog falling, peace, and universal dominion. (Troki, Part I ch. 35)"],
    ["Zechariah 9:1–17","Matthew 21:1–7; 10:34; 20:28","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part I ch. 35"],
    [
      N(cr('troki'),
        "But the peace and dominion of verse 10 are for the Second Coming. The donkey entry was the first fulfilment.",
        "Zechariah gives one coming of one king, with the donkey and the peace together. The text does not split them into two visits. This is the usual way a missed condition gets stretched.",
        "Splitting the prophecy",
        [ "Verses 9 and 10 are one sentence of a single oracle with one king. There is no hint in the text of a first and second visit. (Troki, Part I ch. 35)",
          "Turn the tables · If a prophecy can be fulfilled in two parts, a thousand years apart, then almost any prophecy can be matched afterwards. This is the “shoot the arrow, then draw the circle” problem. (Kravitz’s image, applied here)"],
        ["Zechariah 9:9–10","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part I ch. 35"],
        []
      ),
      N(cr('troki'),
        "Matthew describes Jesus riding a donkey and a colt. Isn’t that a precise fit with “an ass, and a colt the foal of an ass”?",
        "Turn the tables: In Hebrew, “on an ass, even on a colt, the foal of an ass” is one animal said twice, a normal poetic pattern. Matthew has two animals, and the disciples put cloaks “on them”. Mark, Luke and John have only one colt.",
        "Turn the tables · Matthew’s two animals",
        [ "Turn the tables · Matthew 21:2–7 has the disciples bring a donkey and a colt, and Jesus sits “on them”. Mark 11:2, Luke 19:30 and John 12:14 have one colt. (the Gospel texts)",
          "Turn the tables · Zechariah’s phrase is Hebrew parallelism: the colt is the donkey’s foal, the same animal named twice. Matthew seems to read the line as two animals. That shows the writer shaping the event to fit the words, not the words predicting the event. (Zechariah 9:9; Matthew 21:2–7 vs Mark 11:2–7, Luke 19:30–35, John 12:14)"],
        ["Zechariah 9:9","Matthew 21:2–7","Mark 11:2","Luke 19:30","John 12:14"],
        []
      ),
    ]
  ),
  sum:"Zechariah 9 is an end-time prophecy of peace and ingathering, with no horses or chariots. A donkey does not prove who the rider is, and Matthew’s two animals show he is reading the verse his own way."
});

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ MATTHEW'S FULFILMENT */
card('matthew-fulfilment',{
  title:"Matthew’s “that it might be fulfilled”: Egypt, Rachel, Nazarene, Jeremiah",
  k:"Matthew 1–2, 4, 21–22, 27:9–10",
  blurb:"Matthew cites the Old Testament again and again. Check each quotation in its own context.",
  claim:"Matthew shows that Jesus fulfils prophecy: out of Egypt (Hosea 11:1), Rachel weeping (Jeremiah 31:15), a Nazarene, Galilee (Isaiah 9), and the thirty pieces of silver.",
  tree:N(cr('troki','kravitz','cl',DM1),
    "Matthew says again and again that events in Jesus’ life happened “that it might be fulfilled”. Taken together, Egypt, Bethlehem, Nazareth and Galilee form a pattern. Matthew is using typology, where an event in Israel’s history foreshadows Christ.",
    "Check each quotation in its own chapter. Hosea 11:1 is about Israel leaving Egypt. Jeremiah 31:15 is about exile, not Bethlehem. The Nazarene quote is not in the Hebrew Bible. Isaiah 9 is about Assyria. The “Jeremiah” quote is mostly Zechariah. Each time, the verse looks different once you read what comes before and after it.",
    "The Christian case at its strongest, and the core answer",
    [ "Steelman · The pattern is cumulative: Egypt (Hosea 11:1), the slaughter in Bethlehem (Jer 31:15), the Nazarene, Galilee (Isa 9:1–2), and thirty pieces of silver (Zech 11:12–13). Even if each looks small, together they are said to point to Jesus (Troki, Part II ch. 4–8, 25; Kravitz, Proof Texts; Choose Life, Tampering with the Evidence).",
      "Steelman · Matthew is not claiming every verse was a prediction. He uses typology: Israel’s history is a pattern that repeats in the Messiah (Choose Life).",
      "Answer · Kravitz’s test is a parable: shoot the arrow, then draw the circle around it. For each citation ask whether the text was mistranslated, misquoted, taken out of context, or made up. (Kravitz, Refuting Christian Proof Texts, opening parable)",
      "Answer · Choose Life’s summary is that the citations fall in those categories, and that Jews should read the whole Tanakh in Hebrew and in context. (Choose Life, pp. 18, 49)",
      "Answer · The reply to each one is below, from the verses themselves. Isaiah 7:14 (the virgin) has its own card."],
    ["Matthew 1–2, 4, 21–22, 27","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part I ch. 28, 31; Part II ch. 4–8, 25","Kravitz, Jewish Response to Missionaries, Refuting Proof Texts","Choose Life, Tampering with the Evidence pp. 18–23, 49"],
    [
      N(cr('troki','cl'),
        "Hosea 11:1, “out of Egypt have I called my son”: Matthew 2:15 applies it to Jesus.",
        "Hosea is describing Israel’s Exodus. The very next verses say they sacrificed to the Baals. A prediction would not be written in the past tense about an event that already happened.",
        "Out of Egypt (Hosea 11:1)",
        [ "Hosea says that when Israel was a child, God loved him and called his son out of Egypt. This is the Exodus. (Troki, Part II ch. 4)",
          "“Son” is a common description of the Jewish people: “Israel is my son, my firstborn” (Ex 4:22–23). (Troki, Part II ch. 4; Choose Life, Tampering with the Evidence Example #1, p. 20)",
          "Matthew 2:14–15 turns a past event into a messianic prediction. (Choose Life, Example #1, p. 20)",
          "Turn the tables · Christians call this typology, not prediction. But then the verse is not a prophecy that anyone could have used to identify the Messiah in advance, which is what a “proof” would need."],
        ["Hosea 11:1","Exodus 4:22–23","Matthew 2:14–15","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part II ch. 4","Choose Life, Example #1 p. 20"],
        []
      ),
      N(cr('troki'),
        "Jeremiah 31:15, “Rachel weeping for her children”, matches Herod’s killing of the infants of Bethlehem.",
        "In Jeremiah the children are the tribes of Ephraim, taken into exile by Assyria. The next two verses say they will come back from the land of the enemy. Nobody is promised that dead babies return.",
        "Rachel weeping (Jeremiah 31:15)",
        [ "Rachel’s children are the Ephraimite tribes carried off by Assyria (Jer 7:15; Ezek 37:19). (Troki, Part I ch. 28)",
          "Verses 16–17 promise that they “shall come again from the land of the enemy” and return to their border. This fits exile and return, not murdered infants. (Troki, Part I ch. 28; Part II ch. 5)",
          "Bethlehem’s people come from Leah through Judah, not from Rachel. If the verse were about Bethlehem, Leah would weep. (Troki, Part I ch. 28)",
          "The Hebrew word enennu, “he is not”, is singular. It refers to the “people”, the missing ten tribes, not to “they are no more”. (Troki, Part I ch. 28)"],
        ["Jeremiah 31:15–17","Matthew 2:16–18","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part I ch. 28; Part II ch. 5"],
        []
      ),
      N(cr('kravitz','cl','troki'),
        "“He shall be called a Nazarene” (Matthew 2:23) is a prophecy about Jesus’ hometown.",
        "No prophet wrote these words. The Hebrew Bible does not contain “Nazarene”. Matthew says “the prophets”, in the plural, so he points to no specific verse.",
        "“He shall be called a Nazarene”",
        [ "The quotation appears in no prophetic writing, and the words “Nazareth” and “Nazarene” are absent from the Tanakh. (Troki, Part II ch. 6; Choose Life, Example #5, pp. 22–23; Kravitz, Example 1)",
          "A link to nazir (a Nazirite) fails: nazir is spelled with a zayin, and Natzeret with a tsade. (Choose Life, Example #5)",
          "A link to netzer, “branch” (Isa 11:1), shares a root, but it is a different word and not the same quote, and it does not name the town. (Choose Life, Example #5)",
          "Slip fixed: Kravitz says Nazareth did not exist in biblical times, so the “prophecy” was made up. That is an argument from silence, so I leave it out. The stronger point is that the quoted words are not in any prophet. (Kravitz, Example 1, p. 33ff)"],
        ["Matthew 2:23","Isaiah 11:1","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part II ch. 6","Kravitz, Jewish Response to Missionaries Example 1","Choose Life, Example #5 pp. 22–23"],
        []
      ),
      N(cr('troki','cl'),
        "Isaiah 9:1–2, “the land of Zebulun and Naphtali … Galilee”: Matthew 4:13–16 applies it to Jesus in Capernaum.",
        "Isaiah is describing the Assyrian devastation of those lands and the light of deliverance after Sennacherib’s army fell. Matthew quotes the part about light and leaves out the part about the king who afflicted them.",
        "Galilee (Isaiah 8:23–9:2)",
        [ "Isaiah 8 is a historical account of Assyria afflicting Zebulun, Naphtali and Galilee, leading to the exile of the northern tribes. (Choose Life, Example #2, pp. 20–21)",
          "Matthew’s quotation leaves out the clause about the king afflicting these lands, which weakens his claim of a messianic prophecy. (Choose Life, Example #2, pp. 20–21)",
          "The deliverance is the fall of Sennacherib’s army (185,000 killed), and the child of Isaiah 9:5 is Hezekiah, according to Troki. (Troki, Part II ch. 8)"],
        ["Isaiah 8:23–9:6","Matthew 4:13–16","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part II ch. 8","Choose Life, Example #2"],
        []
      ),
      N(cr('troki','cl'),
        "Matthew 27:9–10 says Jeremiah prophesied the thirty pieces of silver.",
        "Jeremiah wrote nothing like that. The closest texts are Jeremiah 32:6–7 (buying a field) and Zechariah 11:12–14 (thirty pieces thrown to the potter), both historical-style passages. Matthew gets the prophet wrong. A separate card covers this in depth.",
        "Thirty pieces of silver",
        [ "Matthew names Jeremiah, but the thirty silver pieces are in Zechariah 11:12–13, and the field in Jeremiah 32:6–7. (Choose Life, Example #6, p. 23; Troki, Part II ch. 25)",
          "Zechariah’s silver and two staves (Beauty and Bands) are figures for Israel’s leaders and fortunes under the second Temple, with no link to Jesus. (Troki, Part II ch. 25)",
          "For the full discussion, see the existing card “Did Jeremiah or Zechariah prophesy Judas’s 30 silver pieces?”."],
        ["Matthew 27:9–10","Zechariah 11:12–13","Jeremiah 32:6–7","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part II ch. 25","Choose Life, Example #6 p. 23"],
        []
      ),
      N(cr('kravitz'),
        "Matthew is only one author. Paul and the other apostles use Isaiah and the Psalms well.",
        "The same problem appears in Paul. Kravitz gives Isaiah 59:20 in Romans 11:26 as an example of a changed quotation.",
        "Quotations changed in the New Testament",
        [ "Romans 11:26 reads “from Zion … to remove ungodliness”. The Hebrew of Isaiah 59:20 says “to Zion”, and “to those who turn from transgression”. In the Hebrew, the redeemer comes in response to repentance, not to remove sin. (Kravitz, Example 2, Mistranslated)",
          "Kravitz notes that many New Testaments render the verse correctly in Isaiah, and differently in Romans. (Kravitz, Example 2)"],
        ["Isaiah 59:20","Romans 11:26","Kravitz, Jewish Response to Missionaries Example 2"],
        []
      ),
      N(cr('troki','kravitz'),
        "Isaiah 7:14 (Matthew 1:22–23) is the first of these, and it is the most famous.",
        "That text is covered in its own card. Matthew’s citation relies on the word “virgin” and ignores Isaiah 7–8, whose context is the Assyrian threat to Ahaz.",
        "Isaiah 7:14",
        [ "See the card on Isaiah 7:14. In short, the Matthew citation depends on the translation “virgin”, and on ignoring the context of Isaiah 7–8. (Kravitz, Example 3)"],
        ["Isaiah 7:14","Matthew 1:22–23","Kravitz, Jewish Response to Missionaries Example 3"],
        []
      ),
      N(cr(DM1),
        "Matthew 21–22 shows the kingdom passing from unbelieving Israel to the Church built on the cornerstone, Jesus.",
        "On the Muslim reading, the “other nation” in the parable of the tenants is not the Church. Read with Daniel 2 and 7 and with 4 Ezra, the kingdom passes to another people who come after Rome. This reading is presented in the sources below, whose content I could not see.",
        "Matthew 21–22: the “other nation”",
        [ "A thread in Islamic Apologetics (#muhammad-ﷺ-in-the-bible, “4 Ezra & Matt 21–22”) shares a PDF (Matthew 21–22 Prophecy) and an article on 4–6 Ezra. They argue that the earliest reading of Matthew 21–22 has the kingdom pass to another people who succeed Rome, in line with Daniel 2 and 7. (Discord, IA #muhammad-ﷺ-in-the-bible)",
          "A second thread (“Matthew 22”) links scans and videos that read the banquet and the tenants as pointing to a further messenger after Jesus, and compares this with Paul’s so-called prophecies. See the card “Matthew 21–22: the kingdom & the banquet” for the full argument. (Discord, IA #muhammad-ﷺ-in-the-bible)",
          "This is the Muslim reading of the passage and not a Jewish argument. It shows how the same chapter that Christians use for fulfilment can be read another way."],
        ["Matthew 21–22","Daniel 2, 7","4 Ezra","Discord IA #muhammad-ﷺ-in-the-bible"],
        []
      ),
    ],
    {visual:{kind:'compare',left:{title:'What Matthew says it fulfils',items:['“Out of Egypt” (Hosea 11:1)','Rachel weeping (Jer 31:15)','“He shall be called a Nazarene”','Galilee of the nations (Isa 9:1–2)','Thirty pieces, “by Jeremiah”']},right:{title:'What the passage is about',items:['Israel leaving Egypt (Ex 4:22)','Exile of Ephraim, who then return (Jer 31:16–17)','No such words in the Tanakh','Assyria, then deliverance (Isa 8–9)','Zechariah 11:12–13; field in Jer 32']}}}
  ),
  sum:"Each of Matthew’s citations looks different in its own context: Hosea is about the Exodus, Jeremiah 31 about exile and return, the Nazarene quote is not in the prophets, Isaiah 9 is about Assyria, and the silver is in Zechariah, not Jeremiah."
});

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ ISAIAH 61 / LUKE 4 */
card('isaiah-61-luke-4',{
  title:"“Today this Scripture is fulfilled” (Isaiah 61 and Luke 4)",
  k:"Isaiah 61:1–2; Luke 4:16–21",
  blurb:"Jesus stops reading in the middle of the sentence. Isaiah speaks of exiled Israel.",
  claim:"Jesus read Isaiah 61:1–2 in the synagogue and said, “This day is this Scripture fulfilled in your ears” (Luke 4:17–21).",
  tree:N(cr('troki'),
    "In the synagogue at Nazareth Jesus read, “The Spirit of the Lord is upon me … to preach good tidings”, and said it was fulfilled that day. So he is the anointed one of Isaiah.",
    "Isaiah is speaking of his own prophetic anointing and of exiled Israel. Luke’s reading is not the same as the Hebrew text: it adds a line and leaves out “the day of vengeance” and the consolation of Zion’s mourners.",
    "The Christian case at its strongest, and the core answer",
    [ "Steelman · Jesus reads the passage aloud, says it is fulfilled that day, and the crowd takes it as a claim about himself (Luke 4:17–21; Troki, Part II ch. 36).",
      "Answer · Luke adds “recovery of sight to the blind” and leaves out “the day of vengeance of our God” and the comfort of those who mourn, with “glory for ashes”. Jesus did not do those things. (Troki, Part II ch. 36)",
      "Answer · “The Lord has anointed me” means prophetic anointing. The afflicted, the captives and the mourners of Zion are exiled Israel, to whom the consolation was addressed. (Troki, Part II ch. 36)",
      "Turn the tables · Luke 4:20 has Jesus close the book mid-sentence, before “the day of vengeance”. Christians say that part is for the Second Coming. But then the passage was not fulfilled “this day” as a whole, so Luke’s “fulfilled” does not match Isaiah’s whole sentence."],
    ["Isaiah 61:1–3","Luke 4:16–21","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part II ch. 36"],
    []
  ),
  sum:"Luke’s quotation of Isaiah 61 omits the day of vengeance and the consolation of Zion. Isaiah speaks as a prophet to exiled Israel."
});

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ PSALM 8 / HEBREWS 2 */
card('psalm-8-hebrews-2',{
  title:"“A little lower than the angels” (Psalm 8 and Hebrews 2)",
  k:"Psalm 8:4–6; Hebrews 2:6–9",
  blurb:"Psalm 8 is about humankind. Hebrews re-applies it to Jesus.",
  claim:"Hebrews 2:7–9 reads Psalm 8:5–6 as being about Jesus, who was “made a little lower than the angels”.",
  tree:N(cr('troki'),
    "Hebrews 2:7–9 applies “thou madest him a little lower than the angels” to Jesus, who was lowered below the angels for a time, and who is now worshipped by them.",
    "Psalm 8 is about human beings. The psalmist marvels that frail humans are given a noble place and rule over creation (compare Gen 1:26). It has nothing to do with Jesus.",
    "The Christian case at its strongest, and the core answer",
    [ "Steelman · Hebrews says the psalm is about the one who was lowered and then crowned with glory and honour, who is Jesus (Heb 2:7–9; Troki, Part II ch. 96).",
      "Answer · The psalm asks “what is man, that thou art mindful of him?” and answers with humanity’s noble state and its rule over the creatures. It is about human beings in general. (Troki, Part II ch. 96)",
      "Answer · Troki finds it strange to speak of someone lower than the angels who is worshipped by them. (Troki, Part II ch. 96)",
      "Turn the tables · Hebrews 2:6 itself begins by quoting “what is man?”. By the author’s own opening, the psalm is about man, and he then shifts it to one man. Being “lower than angels” is also a description of a human, not of God. (adapted from Hebrews 2:6–9)"],
    ["Psalm 8:4–6","Genesis 1:26","Hebrews 2:6–9","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part II ch. 96"],
    []
  ),
  sum:"Psalm 8 is about humanity’s place in creation. Hebrews 2 reapplies it to one person."
});

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ ABRAHAM'S SEED */
card('abraham-seed-gen-22-18',{
  title:"“In your seed all nations shall be blessed” (Genesis 22:18)",
  k:"Genesis 22:17–18; Galatians 3:16",
  blurb:"“Seed” is a collective word in Hebrew. The blessing is for the nations through Israel, not through one man.",
  claim:"Genesis 22:18 refers specifically and exclusively to Jesus as the one “seed” of Abraham, as Paul argues in Galatians 3:16.",
  tree:N(cr('troki'),
    "God promised that in Abraham’s seed all nations would be blessed. Paul points out that it says “seed”, not “seeds”, so it refers to one person, Christ (Gal 3:16).",
    "In Hebrew, “seed” (zera) means descendants and is never plural. The previous verse says the seed is as many as the stars and sand, so it is the whole people. The blessing reaches the nations through Israel’s faith.",
    "The Christian case at its strongest, and the core answer",
    [ "Steelman · Paul’s argument is that the promise names a single seed, and that is Christ (Gal 3:16; Troki, Part I ch. 13).",
      "Answer · The verse before (Gen 22:17) says the seed is as numerous as the stars of heaven and the sand of the shore, so it is the whole people. (Troki, Part I ch. 13)",
      "Answer · The same promise is repeated to Isaac and Jacob (Gen 26:4; 28:14) and to Abraham earlier (12:3; 18:18). Deuteronomy 1:10 applies the star-multitude to Israel. (Troki, Part I ch. 13)",
      "Answer · Other nations are blessed by joining Israel’s faith (Num 10:32; Isa 14:1; 56:6–7; Zech 8:23), and the patriarchs blessed by proclaiming God’s unity. (Troki, Part I ch. 13)",
      "Answer · Zera is a collective noun and is never plural. It is used of many in Genesis 13:15–16 (“thy seed as the dust of the earth”), 15:5 (as the stars) and 15:13. So Paul’s argument that “seed” must mean one person is built on a point of grammar the Hebrew does not support. (Troki, Part II ch. 90)",
      "Turn the tables · Paul himself says in Galatians 3:29, “If ye be Christ’s, then are ye Abraham’s seed”. So he uses “seed” of a group in the same chapter. (adapted from Gal 3:29)",
      "Answer · Troki also finds it extraordinary that a shameful death should free followers from the law (Gal 3:13), since submission, not abandonment, would follow. (Troki, Part II ch. 89)"],
    ["Genesis 22:17–18; 26:4; 28:14; 12:3; 18:18","Deuteronomy 1:10","Galatians 3:13, 16, 29","Troki, Faith Strengthened Part I ch. 13; Part II ch. 89–90"],
    []
  ),
  sum:"“Seed” is a collective noun for Abraham’s descendants, as the verse before shows. Paul’s one-seed argument depends on a grammar point that the Hebrew does not support."
});

/* ------------------------------------------------------------ 1 COR 10:4 */
card('1-corinthians-10-4-rock',{
  title:"“The Rock was Christ” (1 Corinthians 10:4)",
  k:"1 Corinthians 10:4; Exodus 17:6; Numbers 20:11",
  blurb:"A rock that follows Israel is not in the Torah. It comes from later rabbinic tradition.",
  claim:"1 Corinthians 10:4, “that Rock was Christ”, shows Christ was present in the wilderness and the Old Testament points to him.",
  tree:N(cr('singer',DROCK),
    "Paul says Israel drank from a spiritual Rock that followed them, and that the Rock was Christ. So Christ was already present in the Exodus.",
    "The Torah never says the rock followed Israel. Moses strikes a rock at Horeb (Ex 17:6) and another at Kadesh (Num 20:11). The idea of a travelling rock appears in the Talmud and Midrash, so Paul draws on later Jewish tradition, not on the Old Testament text.",
    "The Christian case at its strongest, and the core answer",
    [ "Steelman · Paul says the whole generation drank from the same spiritual drink, from a spiritual rock that went with them, and the rock was Christ (1 Cor 10:4). This is said to show that Christ was active in the Old Testament (Discord, AV #moses-sprechender-stein).",
      "Answer · The Torah mentions only a rock struck at Horeb (Ex 17:6) and one at Kadesh (Num 20:11). It never says a rock moved or followed Israel. (Discord, AV #moses-sprechender-stein)",
      "Answer · The travelling rock is found in the Talmud (Ta’anit 9a) and in the Midrash (Bamidbar Rabbah 1:2). So “a spiritual rock that followed them” is borrowed from later tradition. (Discord, AV #moses-sprechender-stein)",
      "Answer · If Paul’s claim is based on a tradition that is not in the Old Testament, it does not show that the Old Testament foretells Jesus. (Discord, AV #moses-sprechender-stein)"],
    ["1 Corinthians 10:4","Exodus 17:6","Numbers 20:11","Talmud, Ta’anit 9a","Bamidbar Rabbah 1:2","Discord AV #moses-sprechender-stein (translated from German)"],
    []
  ),
  sum:"The rock that follows Israel is not in the Torah. It comes from later rabbinic tradition, which Paul uses, so 1 Corinthians 10:4 does not show the Old Testament predicting Christ."
});
})();
